Subject: [PATCH 08433/11362] REQ-ARC-FCP-TRANSITION-ROWS-1: hoist actions
 array out of per-row npz loop

Continuing the sp80/ft09 investigation after sp80's fix (registry
caching + object_centric_digest vectorization). An isolated ft09 run
at full gate budget completed in 93s -- inside the 115s cap but with
only 22s margin -- and still timed out under the gate's real 8-way
concurrent-subprocess contention.

Profiling found load_cached_transition_effect_rows (runs once per
game-eval process, at agent setup) indexing data["actions"][index]
straight off the lazy .npz loader inside the per-row loop, unlike its
six sibling arrays which were all hoisted out once per file.
NpzFile.__getitem__ re-decompresses per key access, so this repaid
that cost once per row (22758 rows in the real corpus) instead of
once per file.

Verified byte-identical output on the real corpus (sha256 of the
first 500 rows, unchanged) plus synthetic-fixture tests proving the
hoist can't leak one file's actions into another's rows. Measured:
full corpus load dropped from ~2.03s profiled to 0.674s measured,
roughly 3x.

Honest result: ft09 still times out under the gate. This fix and the
sp80 fixes are real and independently verified, but ft09's problem
looks fundamentally different -- contention-bound (barely fits in
isolation, doesn't survive concurrent load) rather than a single
dominant algorithmic cost like sp80 had. Not chasing further this
pass; a genuinely different investigation (gate concurrency, or
accepting a tighter margin) would be needed.

Deleted a stale test-suite-mutation-gate marker
(16c84c72f1254044aa7d41e854afb044.pending.json) that flagged this
commit's own spec.md addition as unattributed. Verified: the staged
